  spec: [
    'Reasons for measuring fitness and health: strengths and weaknesses, baseline, current state, improvement, comparisons, motivation',
    'Fitness tests linked to components: multi-stage fitness test/Cooper 12-minute run, abdominal curl/press-up test, hand grip/1RM, sit and reach, skinfold callipers, Illinois agility run, 30/50 m sprint, vertical jump, stork balance, alternate-hand throw, ruler drop',
    'Other measurement tools: health questionnaires, screening, blood pressure, heart rate, calorie input and expenditure',
    'The importance of protocols; reliability and validity'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Why measure fitness?', html: `
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>Identify strengths and weaknesses</td><td>Shows which components need training.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Baseline / current state</td><td>A starting point to plan training and set targets.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monitor improvement</td><td>Retest after training to see whether it worked.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Comparisons</td><td>With previous results, team-mates or <b>national norms</b> (average scores for age and sex).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motivation</td><td>Seeing progress, or a target to beat, encourages effort and adherence.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Design a programme</td><td>Choose the right methods and intensity.</td></tr></table></div>` },
    { h: 'The fitness tests', html: `
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Component</th><th>Test</th><th>Protocol (summary)</th><th>Result</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cardiovascular endurance</td><td><b>Multi-stage fitness test</b> (bleep test)</td><td>Run 20 m shuttles in time with bleeps that get faster; stop when you miss two in a row.</td><td>Level and shuttle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cardiovascular endurance</td><td><b>Cooper 12-minute run</b></td><td>Run as far as possible round a track in 12 min.</td><td>Distance (m)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muscular endurance</td><td><b>Abdominal curl</b> / <b>press-up test</b></td><td>As many correct reps as possible (to a beat, or in 1 min).</td><td>Number of reps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strength</td><td><b>Hand grip dynamometer</b> / <b>1 rep max</b></td><td>Squeeze as hard as possible (best of 3, dominant hand) / the heaviest weight lifted once.</td><td>kg</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flexibility</td><td><b>Sit and reach</b></td><td>Legs straight, reach forward slowly along the box; hold 2 s.</td><td>cm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Body composition</td><td><b>Skinfold callipers</b> / body fat monitor</td><td>Measure skinfolds at set sites (e.g. biceps, triceps, subscapular, suprailiac).</td><td>mm → % body fat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Agility</td><td><b>Illinois agility run</b></td><td>Start lying face down; sprint a set course weaving round cones.</td><td>Time (s)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speed</td><td><b>30 m / 50 m sprint</b></td><td>Flying or standing start; sprint flat out.</td><td>Time (s)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power</td><td><b>Vertical jump</b></td><td>Mark standing reach, jump as high as possible; difference.</td><td>cm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Balance</td><td><b>Stork balance</b></td><td>Stand on one foot, other foot against the knee, rise onto toes; time until balance lost.</td><td>Time (s)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Co-ordination</td><td><b>Alternate-hand wall throw</b></td><td>Throw a ball against a wall 2 m away with one hand, catch with the other, for 30 s.</td><td>Catches</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reaction time</td><td><b>Ruler drop test</b></td><td>Partner drops a ruler; catch it as quickly as possible.</td><td>cm (shorter = faster)</td></tr></table></div>
` },
    { h: 'Other health measures', html: `
<ul>
<li><b>Health questionnaires</b> (e.g. PAR-Q) — screen for medical conditions, injuries and lifestyle before starting exercise.</li>
<li><b>Blood pressure</b> — measured with a cuff; around 120/80 mmHg is normal at rest. High readings show hypertension risk.</li>
<li><b>Resting heart rate</b> — a lower resting HR usually shows a fitter heart. Recovery heart rate after exercise also shows fitness.</li>
<li><b>Calorie input and expenditure</b> — food diaries and activity trackers show energy balance.</li>
</ul>` },
    { h: 'Protocols, reliability and validity', html: `
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>How to improve it</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Protocol</b></td><td>The standard set of instructions for carrying out a test.</td><td>Follow it exactly every time.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Reliability</b></td><td>Whether the test gives consistent results when repeated.</td><td>Same time of day, same surface, same tester, same warm-up, calibrated equipment, several trials.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Validity</b></td><td>Whether the test measures what it claims to measure.</td><td>Choose a test that matches the component and the sport (e.g. a swimmer’s endurance tested in the pool, not on a track).</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="box warn"><b class="lbl">Limitations of tests</b><p>Maximal tests (bleep test) depend on motivation; sit and reach only tests the hamstrings and lower back; skinfold readings depend on the tester’s skill; tests are not sport-specific; weather affects outdoor tests.</p></div>` }
  ],
